{"artifact_id":"Figure7","kind":"figure","asset_path":"figures/fig_2606.11169v1-Figure7-1-7d453e34ac2a.png","asset_sha256":"7d453e34ac2a2239655b193fbfede58e892fac193f3747c1c9b3d2f6ef211fc6","source_pdf_sha256":"30499cad3af2d2b8042e5421e02e540fa4a90ac89061f2938d4674630b7bf48d","revision_vid":"264a2ac4762211b08faff46a62158c351fa6737a7e972f96b79c6bbbc3e461ea","canonical_id":"2606.11169","section_vid":"07f5c7a1399e6f6c4091ce258164b267d5e7f91d81aa0d8e4c67f659aa73c63f","detector_fingerprint":"pdffigures2-0.0.12-SNAPSHOT","detector_metadata_sha256":"bdd8b1bfbb77e67ca9c27df8b1deddbb19246eb64777a7735e6dea9dca1b1f4d","page_index":9,"region":{"x1":316.8,"y1":70.56,"x2":558.24,"y2":163.2},"caption":"Figure 7. PP x EP throughput for 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B and DualPipeV schedules.","containing_section":{"relation":"containing","section_id":"sec-1~23","path_id":"1~23","title":"Figure 7 .","text":"7Figure 7. PP x EP throughput for 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B and DualPipeV schedules.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":38},"neighboring_sections":[{"relation":"previous","section_id":"sec-1~22","path_id":"1~22","title":"1","text":"PPStr, EPStr, DPStr = sys.stream(), sys.stream(), sys.stream() 2 Place((PP=0), device=[0,2], stream=PPStr) 3 Place((PP=1), device=[1,3], stream=PPStr) 4 Replicate((PP=0, EP=-), devices=[0,2], reduce_stream=DPStr) 5 Replicate((PP=1, EP=-), devices=[1,3], reduce_stream=DPStr) 6 Shard((PP=0, EP=*), devices=[0,2], stream=EPStr) 7 Shard((PP=1, EP=*), devices=[1,3], stream=EPStr) 8 MB = \"microbatch\" 9 Split((), dim=MB, num_microbatches=2) 10 Order([(PP=0, MB=0, PASS=F), (PP=0, MB=1, PASS=F), (PP=0, MB=0, PASS=B), (PP=0, MB=1, PASS=B)]) 11 Order([(PP=1, MB=0, PASS=F), [(PP=1, MB=1, PASS=F), (PP=1, MB=0, PASS=B)], (PP=1, MB=1, PASS=B)])","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":37},{"relation":"next","section_id":"sec-1~24","path_id":"1~24","title":"Figure 8 .","text":"8Figure 8. Peak Memory consumption of ZeRO-2 and ZeRO-3 sharding strategies for Piper and TorchTitan. full copy of parameters and gradients across multiple microbatches and OOMs at batch size 8 for ZeRO-2 and batch size 16 for ZeRO-3. TorchTitan's PP x ZeRO-3 implementation also does not save significant memory compared to ZeRO-2.For Piper, we achieve correct resharding by reducing gradients after every backward pass instead of accumulating gradients between PP microbatches. This adds communication time compared to TorchTitan but achieves the full ZeRO memory savings. As a result, Piper is able to execute with much higher batch sizes than TorchTitan without OOM: 8x higher for ZeRO-2 (up to batch size 32) and 3.3x higher for ZeRO-3 (up to batch size 40).","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":39}],"reference_excerpts":[{"section_id":"sec-6.3","path_id":"6.3","title":"PP x EP and DualPipe","matched_form":"figure 7","excerpt":"n each case is from overlapping of some EP communication. TorchTitan's DualPipeV implementation uses separate threads to dispatch the overlapped forward and backward microbatches to two streams, one for compute and one for EP communication.We adapt the DualPipeV schedule-builder from TorchTitan to the Piper API in 63 LoC. We use the Order directive to specify microbatch overlapping. Similar to TorchTitan, the runtime also uses a background stream for EP communication, but the tasks from the overlapped microbatches are scheduled jointly by a single thread.We use the same experiment setup as in Figure 7a. In the 1B setting, Piper-DualPipeV improves 13% over Piper-1F1B schedule, while TorchTitan-DualPipeV improves only 3% over TorchTitan-1F1B. We believe that TorchTitan's lack of improvement is due to a lack of synchronization between the forwards and backwards dispatch threads, which leads to unintended serialization of the GPU's compute and communication streams.For the 9B model, we were not able to run TorchTitan due to OOM. Piper-DualPipeV improves 10% over its interleaved schedule and 6% over Megatron's interleaved schedule (Figure 7b. In this case, Megatron-Interleaved-1F1B's im","truncated":true},{"section_id":"sec-1~23","path_id":"1~23","title":"Figure 7 .","matched_form":"figure 7","excerpt":"7Figure 7. PP x EP throughput for 1F1B, Interleaved 1F1B and DualPipeV schedules.","truncated":false}],"article_summary":{"source_id":"grobid:2606.11169#abstract","source_text_sha256":"890e7f054301875698f7006f65bac813b121ea4ed20d841fb83163c58f6ae4d4","text":"Large-scale model training increasingly relies on composing multiple parallelism strategies, such as data, pipeline, and expert parallelism, together with memory-saving optimizations like ZeRO. Deployed systems for foundation model pretraining often rely on human experts to manually design a highlevel parallelism strategy then implement the corresponding low-level execution strategy, making it difficult to adapt the system to new strategies. Meanwhile, many general-purpose frameworks are more flexible but their implementations are still tied to a fixed set of common parallelism strategies, making it challenging to integrate state-of-the-art strategies.We present Piper, a user-controllable distributed training system that decouples the strategy from the runtime implementation. Piper allows users to declare a comprehensive distributed training strategy with a small set of model annotations and scheduling directives. Each directive applies a transformation on Piper's intermediate representation (IR), a unified global training DAG that represents all computation and communication. Using this IR, Piper compiles per-device execution plans and executes them with a distributed runtime agnostic to the strategy. We show that the combined system maintains performance parity on commonly available strategies such as ZeRO, while also enabling additional performance and memory efficiency gains through joint scheduling of compute and communication in composed parallelism strategies such as DeepSeek-V3's DualPipe.","truncated":false},"diagnostics":[],"total_context_chars":4363,"truncated":true}